; regression test for a real bug found while implementing selective export: an alias is pure
; syntax sugar for an existing symbol -- axioms are written with the *canonical* name (e.g.
; `not`), so an alias (`¬`) never itself "occurs" in any formula's free symbols. The first
; version of the export-closure computation missed this entirely, so `¬` silently lost its
; alias registration on `load` even though `not` itself (the alias's target) survived fine --
; confirmed by a real failure: `proofs/natural-deduction/neg-forall.kurt` (which `load`s
; `logic`, transitively `prop`, and writes `¬P(x)`) misparsed as soon as this closure ran.
; Minimal repro here: `¬` must still work as `not`'s alias after `load prop`.
load prop
bool P
use ¬¬P implies P
theory
